        "correct": 1,
        "explanation": "The passage states that in the sponge condition, microRNA-29b activity was inhibited. Sponges are commonly used to sequester microRNAs and prevent them from binding to their targets, thereby inhibiting their function. Thus, option B is correct. Option A is the opposite. Option C is not accurate; the sponge is an experimental manipulation, not a control. Option D is not the purpose of the sponge; the passage indicates that microRNA-29b promotes neural epithelial differentiation, so inhibiting it would likely reduce that, not induce it.",
        "difficulty": "medium",
        "section": "Biological & Biochemical Foundations",
        "topic": "The Cell",
        "subject": "Biology",
        "passage": "The development of a multicellular organism from a single fertilized egg requires a precisely orchestrated sequence of events whereby cells acquire specialized structures and functions. This process, known as differentiation, is the final step in a cell's commitment to a particular lineage. Prior to differentiation, a cell may undergo determination, a process in which its developmental fate becomes fixed, even though the cell may not yet exhibit the morphological or biochemical characteristics of its final cell type. Once determined, a cell is committed to a specific path, and its subsequent differentiation involves the expression of genes that endow it with the appropriate structure and function.\n\nCells that have not yet undergone terminal differentiation, and that retain the ability to give rise to other cell types, are termed stem cells. Stem cells are found both in embryonic tissues and in various adult tissues, where they contribute to tissue maintenance and repair. The range of cell types into which a stem cell can differentiate is described by its potency. Potency exists along a spectrum, but is often categorized into discrete levels for conceptual clarity. The most versatile stem cells are totipotent cells, which are capable of giving rise to every cell type in the organism, including the extraembryonic tissues such as the placenta. In mammals, only the zygote and the cells of the early morula (up to the 16-cell stage) are considered totipotent. As development proceeds, the morula undergoes compaction and the cells segregate into two populations: the inner cell mass, which will form the embryo proper, and the trophoblast, which will contribute to the placenta. Cells of the inner cell mass are termed pluripotent, as they can differentiate into any cell type of the three germ layers (ectoderm, mesoderm, endoderm) but not into trophoblast-derived tissues. As development continues, pluripotent cells give rise to more restricted progenitors. For example, hematopoietic stem cells are multipotent: they can differentiate into all the various blood cell types—erythrocytes, leukocytes, and platelets—but cannot become neurons, muscle cells, or skin cells. This hierarchy illustrates the progressive restriction of developmental potential.\n\nCell differentiation is influenced heavily by the local microenvironment. A developing cell receives signals from surrounding organizing cells, which are often referred to as inducers, and the cell that responds to such signals is called a responder. These signals can be secreted molecules, such as growth factors, or membrane-bound proteins that engage receptors on adjacent cells. The response of a cell to an inducing signal depends on its competence—its ability to respond to a given signal at a particular time. This dynamic interplay between inducers and responders ensures that cells differentiate appropriately according to their position within the embryo.\n\nRecent research has highlighted the role of microRNAs in modulating differentiation. A study on neuroectoderm cells, which are derived from ectoderm and can give rise to neural epithelial cells or neural crest cells, investigated the function of microRNA-29b. The researchers used two experimental conditions: a 'sponge' condition in which microRNA-29b activity was inhibited, and an overexpression condition in which microRNA-29b was present in excess. The results, shown in Figure 1, demonstrate that inhibiting microRNA-29b increased differentiation towards the neural crest lineage, whereas overexpressing it reduced neural crest differentiation. In contrast, microRNA-29b was found to promote differentiation into neural epithelial cells (data not shown). These findings indicate that microRNA-29b acts as a molecular switch, suppressing the neural crest fate while promoting the neural epithelial fate. This study illustrates the fine-tuned control that cells exert over their developmental decisions, often through the action of small non-coding RNAs.",
        "figure": "<svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 640 400\" width=\"100%\" style=\"max-width:640px\" role=\"img\" aria-label=\"Neural Crest Differentiation\">\n<rect x=\"0\" y=\"0\" width=\"640\" height=\"400\" fill=\"#ffffff\"/>\n<text x=\"320\" y=\"26\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-family=\"Arial\" font-size=\"15\" font-weight=\"bold\" fill=\"#2b2320\">Neural Crest Differentiation</text>\n<line x1=\"72\" y1=\"334.0\" x2=\"616\" y2=\"334.0\" stroke=\"#e8e2da\" stroke-width=\"1\"/>\n<text x=\"64\" y=\"338.0\" text-anchor=\"end\" font-family=\"Arial\" font-size=\"11\" fill=\"#777\">0</text>\n<line x1=\"72\" y1=\"278.8\" x2=\"616\" y2=\"278.8\" stroke=\"#e8e2da\" stroke-width=\"1\"/>\n<text x=\"64\" y=\"282.8\" text-anchor=\"end\" font-family=\"Arial\" font-size=\"11\" fill=\"#777\">32</text>\n<line x1=\"72\" y1=\"223.6\" x2=\"616\" y2=\"223.6\" stroke=\"#e8e2da\" stroke-width=\"1\"/>\n<text x=\"64\" y=\"227.6\" text-anchor=\"end\" font-family=\"Arial\" font-size=\"11\" fill=\"#777\">64</text>\n<line x1=\"72\" y1=\"168.4\" x2=\"616\" y2=\"168.4\" stroke=\"#e8e2da\" stroke-width=\"1\"/>\n<text x=\"64\" y=\"172.4\" text-anchor=\"end\" font-family=\"Arial\" font-size=\"11\" fill=\"#777\">96</text>\n<line x1=\"72\" y1=\"113.2\" x2=\"616\" y2=\"113.2\" stroke=\"#e8e2da\" stroke-width=\"1\"/>\n<text x=\"64\" y=\"117.2\" text-anchor=\"end\" font-family=\"Arial\" font-size=\"11\" fill=\"#777\">128</text>\n<line x1=\"72\" y1=\"58.0\" x2=\"616\" y2=\"58.0\" stroke=\"#e8e2da\" stroke-width=\"1\"/>\n<text x=\"64\" y=\"62.0\" text-anchor=\"end\" font-family=\"Arial\" font-size=\"11\" fill=\"#777\">160</text>\n<text x=\"96.7\" y=\"352.0\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-family=\"Arial\" font-size=\"11\" fill=\"#777\">Control</text>\n<text x=\"344.0\" y=\"352.0\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-family=\"Arial\" font-size=\"11\" fill=\"#777\">Sponge</text>\n<text x=\"591.3\" y=\"352.0\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-family=\"Arial\" font-size=\"11\" fill=\"#777\">Overexpression</text>\n<line x1=\"72\" y1=\"334\" x2=\"616\" y2=\"334\" stroke=\"#2b2320\" stroke-width=\"1.5\"/>\n<line x1=\"72\" y1=\"58\" x2=\"72\" y2=\"334\" stroke=\"#2b2320\" stroke-width=\"1.5\"/>\n<rect x=\"31.4\" y=\"161.5\" width=\"117.5\" height=\"172.5\" fill=\"#e07a3f\"/>\n<rect x=\"278.7\" y=\"75.2\" width=\"117.5\" height=\"258.8\" fill=\"#e07a3f\"/>\n<rect x=\"526.0\" y=\"247.8\" width=\"117.5\" height=\"86.2\" fill=\"#e07a3f\"/>\n<text x=\"344\" y=\"382\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-family=\"Arial\" font-size=\"13\" fill=\"#2b2320\">Condition</text>\n<text x=\"20\" y=\"196\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-family=\"Arial\" font-size=\"13\" fill=\"#2b2320\" transform=\"rotate(-90 20 196)\">Relative Differentiation (%)</text>\n</svg>",
