        "topic": "Kinematics and Dynamics",
        "subject": "Physics",
        "passage": "The principles of work and energy form a cornerstone of classical mechanics, providing powerful tools for analyzing the motion of objects when forces are not constant or when the path is complex. Work is defined as the product of the force applied to an object and the displacement of the object in the direction of the force. Mathematically, for a constant force F applied at an angle θ to the displacement d, the work done is W = Fd cos θ. When multiple forces act on an object, the net work done on the object equals the change in its kinetic energy, a relationship known as the work–energy theorem: W_net = ΔK = K_f – K_i, where K = ½mv². This theorem is particularly useful because it bypasses the need to calculate acceleration and time when only initial and final speeds are known. For example, consider a car of mass 1200 kg that accelerates from rest to 30 m/s over a distance of 150 m on a straight, horizontal road. The net work done on the car can be found directly from the change in kinetic energy, yielding 540,000 J. If the engine provides a constant horizontal force, that force would be 3600 N. However, real-world situations often involve nonconservative forces such as friction, which dissipate mechanical energy. The work done by nonconservative forces equals the change in total mechanical energy: W_nonconservative = ΔE = ΔU + ΔK. This is essential when analyzing systems where friction or air resistance cannot be neglected. In addition to work and energy, the concept of power describes the rate at which work is performed or energy is transferred. Average power is defined as work divided by time, P = W/t, and is measured in watts (1 W = 1 J/s). For a machine that lifts a mass at constant speed, the power required depends on the force needed to overcome gravity and the speed of lifting. For instance, an elevator motor that lifts a 1000 kg load at a constant speed of 2 m/s must exert an upward force equal to the weight of the load, approximately 9800 N, and thus delivers about 19,600 W of power. Simple machines, such as levers, pulleys, and inclined planes, exploit the conservation of energy to provide mechanical advantage. The mechanical advantage (MA) of a machine is the ratio of the output force to the input force, and for an ideal machine it equals the ratio of the input distance to the output distance. In practice, machines are not perfectly efficient due to friction, and efficiency is defined as the ratio of useful work output to total work input. Figure 1 illustrates the relationship between the force required to lift a constant load using a frictionless inclined plane as a function of the plane's angle of inclination. The data points show a clear trend: as the angle increases, the required force increases because the component of gravity along the plane grows. This graphical representation underscores the trade-off between force and distance inherent in simple machines: a smaller angle allows a smaller force to lift the same load, but the load must be pushed over a longer distance. The data in Figure 1 can be used to verify that the product of force and distance (work) remains constant for a given height, consistent with the conservation of energy. Understanding these interlinked concepts—work, energy, power, and mechanical advantage—enables physicists and engineers to design efficient systems, from cranes to roller coasters, and to predict the motion of objects in a wide range of scenarios.",
        "figure": "<svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 640 400\" width=\"100%\" style=\"max-width:640px\" role=\"img\" aria-label=\"Force vs. Angle for Inclined Plane\">\n<rect x=\"0\" y=\"0\" width=\"640\" height=\"400\" fill=\"#ffffff\"/>\n<text x=\"320\" y=\"26\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-family=\"Arial\" font-size=\"15\" font-weight=\"bold\" fill=\"#2b2320\">Force vs. Angle for Inclined Plane</text>\n<line x1=\"72\" y1=\"334.0\" x2=\"616\" y2=\"334.0\" stroke=\"#e8e2da\" stroke-width=\"1\"/>\n<text x=\"64\" y=\"338.0\" text-anchor=\"end\" font-family=\"Arial\" font-size=\"11\" fill=\"#777\">0</text>\n<line x1=\"72\" y1=\"278.8\" x2=\"616\" y2=\"278.8\" stroke=\"#e8e2da\" stroke-width=\"1\"/>\n<text x=\"64\" y=\"282.8\" text-anchor=\"end\" font-family=\"Arial\" font-size=\"11\" fill=\"#777\">215.6</text>\n<line x1=\"72\" y1=\"223.6\" x2=\"616\" y2=\"223.6\" stroke=\"#e8e2da\" stroke-width=\"1\"/>\n<text x=\"64\" y=\"227.6\" text-anchor=\"end\" font-family=\"Arial\" font-size=\"11\" fill=\"#777\">431.2</text>\n<line x1=\"72\" y1=\"168.4\" x2=\"616\" y2=\"168.4\" stroke=\"#e8e2da\" stroke-width=\"1\"/>\n<text x=\"64\" y=\"172.4\" text-anchor=\"end\" font-family=\"Arial\" font-size=\"11\" fill=\"#777\">646.8</text>\n<line x1=\"72\" y1=\"113.2\" x2=\"616\" y2=\"113.2\" stroke=\"#e8e2da\" stroke-width=\"1\"/>\n<text x=\"64\" y=\"117.2\" text-anchor=\"end\" font-family=\"Arial\" font-size=\"11\" fill=\"#777\">862.4</text>\n<line x1=\"72\" y1=\"58.0\" x2=\"616\" y2=\"58.0\" stroke=\"#e8e2da\" stroke-width=\"1\"/>\n<text x=\"64\" y=\"62.0\" text-anchor=\"end\" font-family=\"Arial\" font-size=\"11\" fill=\"#777\">1.1e+03</text>\n<text x=\"72.0\" y=\"352.0\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-family=\"Arial\" font-size=\"11\" fill=\"#777\">-4.5</text>\n<text x=\"180.8\" y=\"352.0\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-family=\"Arial\" font-size=\"11\" fill=\"#777\">15.3</text>\n<text x=\"289.6\" y=\"352.0\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-family=\"Arial\" font-size=\"11\" fill=\"#777\">35.1</text>\n<text x=\"398.4\" y=\"352.0\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-family=\"Arial\" font-size=\"11\" fill=\"#777\">54.9</text>\n<text x=\"507.2\" y=\"352.0\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-family=\"Arial\" font-size=\"11\" fill=\"#777\">74.7</text>\n<text x=\"616.0\" y=\"352.0\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-family=\"Arial\" font-size=\"11\" fill=\"#777\">94.5</text>\n<line x1=\"72\" y1=\"334\" x2=\"616\" y2=\"334\" stroke=\"#2b2320\" stroke-width=\"1.5\"/>\n<line x1=\"72\" y1=\"58\" x2=\"72\" y2=\"334\" stroke=\"#2b2320\" stroke-width=\"1.5\"/>\n<polyline points=\"96.7,334.0 151.7,290.5 206.6,248.2 261.6,208.5 316.5,172.7 371.5,142.0 426.4,116.4 481.4,98.5 536.3,86.9 591.3,83.1\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#e07a3f\" stroke-width=\"2.5\" stroke-linejoin=\"round\"/>\n<circle cx=\"96.7\" cy=\"334.0\" r=\"3.5\" fill=\"#e07a3f\"/>\n<circle cx=\"151.7\" cy=\"290.5\" r=\"3.5\" fill=\"#e07a3f\"/>\n<circle cx=\"206.6\" cy=\"248.2\" r=\"3.5\" fill=\"#e07a3f\"/>\n<circle cx=\"261.6\" cy=\"208.5\" r=\"3.5\" fill=\"#e07a3f\"/>\n<circle cx=\"316.5\" cy=\"172.7\" r=\"3.5\" fill=\"#e07a3f\"/>\n<circle cx=\"371.5\" cy=\"142.0\" r=\"3.5\" fill=\"#e07a3f\"/>\n<circle cx=\"426.4\" cy=\"116.4\" r=\"3.5\" fill=\"#e07a3f\"/>\n<circle cx=\"481.4\" cy=\"98.5\" r=\"3.5\" fill=\"#e07a3f\"/>\n<circle cx=\"536.3\" cy=\"86.9\" r=\"3.5\" fill=\"#e07a3f\"/>\n<circle cx=\"591.3\" cy=\"83.1\" r=\"3.5\" fill=\"#e07a3f\"/>\n<text x=\"344\" y=\"382\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-family=\"Arial\" font-size=\"13\" fill=\"#2b2320\">Angle (degrees)</text>\n<text x=\"20\" y=\"196\" text-anchor=\"middle\" font-family=\"Arial\" font-size=\"13\" fill=\"#2b2320\" transform=\"rotate(-90 20 196)\">Force (N)</text>\n</svg>",
        "figure_caption": "Force required to lift a 100 kg load up a frictionless inclined plane as a function of the plane's angle of inclination.",
