        "passage": "Memory researchers have long sought to understand how information is processed and stored in the human brain. A foundational model, proposed by Atkinson and Shiffrin in 1968, describes memory as a sequence of three distinct stores: sensory memory, short-term memory, and long-term memory. According to this model, information from the environment first enters sensory memory, where it is held for a very brief period—typically less than a second for visual information (iconic memory) and a few seconds for auditory information (echoic memory). Sensory memory has a large capacity but rapidly decays unless attention is directed to the information, which then transfers it to short-term memory. Short-term memory (STM), also referred to as working memory when its active manipulation is emphasized, holds a limited amount of information—about seven items (plus or minus two)—for approximately 20 seconds. Through processes such as rehearsal and elaborative encoding, information in STM can be transferred to long-term memory (LTM), which has essentially unlimited capacity and can store information for a lifetime. This three-store model, while influential, has been refined and challenged over the years, leading to deeper insights into the nature of memory processes.\n\nTo investigate the distinctions between short-term and long-term memory, a research team conducted a study involving 60 participants: 20 young adults (ages 18–25), 20 older adults (ages 60–75), and 20 patients with mild Alzheimer’s disease (AD). Participants were presented with a list of 20 common words (e.g., 'apple,' 'table,' 'river') one at a time on a computer screen, each for two seconds. After the presentation, participants performed a recall task in which they were asked to write down as many words as they could remember immediately (free recall). Following a 20-minute delay filled with a distractor task (counting backward by threes), participants were again asked to recall the list. The researchers recorded the number of words correctly recalled in each condition. The results are displayed in Figure 1. For young adults, immediate recall averaged 14 words (out of 20), and delayed recall averaged 12 words. Older adults recalled an average of 11 words immediately and 8 words after the delay. In contrast, Alzheimer’s patients recalled only 6 words immediately and 2 words after the delay. The data show a clear pattern: while all groups recalled fewer words after the delay, the decline was most pronounced in the AD group, suggesting that deficits in transferring information from short-term to long-term memory may be a hallmark of Alzheimer’s disease.\n\nThe researchers also administered a recognition test after the delayed recall, in which participants were shown a list of 40 words—the original 20 plus 20 new distractors—and asked to indicate which words they had seen before. In this test, young adults correctly recognized 18 of the 20 original words, older adults recognized 15, and AD patients recognized 9. Interestingly, when asked to rate their confidence in their recognition decisions, AD patients expressed high confidence even for incorrect choices, suggesting a potential impairment in metamemory, the ability to monitor one’s own memory processes.\n\nThese findings align with the broader understanding that memory is not a monolithic entity but rather comprises multiple systems that can be differentially affected by aging and pathology. For instance, the impaired delayed recall in AD patients likely reflects problems with consolidation, the process by which short-term memories are stabilized into long-term storage, which is known to depend heavily on the hippocampus and surrounding medial temporal lobe structures. In contrast, the relatively intact immediate recall in older adults compared to AD patients suggests that sensory and short-term memory processes may be less affected by normal aging. The study underscores the importance of distinguishing between different memory stores and processes, as such distinctions have diagnostic and therapeutic implications for conditions like Alzheimer’s disease.",
