        "explanation": "The passage concludes that the viewer's task is to engage with the work 'bringing one's own experiences to bear,' which suggests that personal engagement enriches meaning. Option C aligns with this. Option A is contradicted by the passage's balanced view that art has multiple functions. Option B is contradicted by the emphasis on viewer interpretation. Option D is not supported; the passage argues for personal engagement, not expert training.",
        "difficulty": "hard",
        "section": "Critical Analysis and Reasoning Skills",
        "topic": "Art History and Aesthetics",
        "subject": "Critical Analysis and Reasoning Skills (CARS)",
        "passage": "The impulse to create images is as old as humanity itself, and yet the question of what art is for remains as contentious as ever. In the caves of Lascaux, our ancestors painted animals with a vitality that still moves us today. Were these paintings merely records of hunts, or did they serve a ritual purpose, perhaps to ensure success in the hunt? The ambiguity is instructive: art rarely has a single function, and its meaning shifts with the eyes that behold it. This fluidity of meaning is not a flaw but a fundamental feature of aesthetic experience.\n\nConsider the Renaissance, a period often celebrated as the pinnacle of artistic achievement. Works like Leonardo's \"Mona Lisa\" and Michelangelo's \"David\" are admired for their technical mastery and their embodiment of humanist ideals. Yet these masterpieces were not created in a cultural vacuum. They were commissioned by powerful patrons—princes, popes, and merchant families—who used art to project their own status and values. The serene smile of the Mona Lisa may delight us today, but for its original owner, it was also a symbol of prestige, a demonstration of wealth and taste. Thus, art serves both as an expression of individual genius and as a tool for social and political ends.\n\nIn the modern era, artists have often turned a critical eye on the very institutions that support them. The Dada movement, born out of the trauma of World War I, rejected the rationality and order that many believed had led to the catastrophe. Marcel Duchamp's infamous \"Fountain\"—a urinal signed with a pseudonym—challenged the very definition of art. By presenting an ordinary object as art, Duchamp forced viewers to confront their assumptions about creativity and the role of the artist. If a mass-produced object could be art, then what was the difference between art and life? This questioning spirit persists in contemporary art, from the political provocations of Ai Weiwei to the identity explorations of Cindy Sherman.\n\nYet, as art becomes ever more conceptual, the question of accessibility arises. If art is only for the initiated—those who understand the theoretical underpinnings of a work—does it lose its power to speak to a broader audience? The public's bafflement at abstract or installation art is often attributed to a lack of education, but perhaps it reflects a deeper disconnect. Art that is too self-referential risks becoming insular, a conversation among elites that excludes the very people it purports to represent. On the other hand, art that panders to popular taste risks becoming mere entertainment, losing its capacity to challenge and provoke.\n\nPerhaps the most honest answer is that art has never been one thing, and its value lies precisely in its multiplicity. A cave painting, a Renaissance portrait, and a Dada readymade all operate on different registers, yet each opens a window onto the human condition. They invite us to see the world anew, to question our assumptions, and to connect with others across time and space. The task of the viewer is not to find a single correct interpretation, but to engage with the work in all its complexity, bringing one's own experiences to bear. In this sense, art is not a fixed object but an ongoing conversation—a conversation that enriches us precisely because it defies easy answers."
